	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85216609203?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The real-time segmentation of surgical instruments plays a crucial role in robot-assisted surgery. However, it is still a challenging task to implement deep learning models to do real-time segmentation for surgical instruments due to substantial expertise and time required for image labeling. The emerging Segment Anything Model (SAM) exhibits impressive capabilities in zero-shot segmentation for natural images. In this paper, we propose an Multi-Feature SAM (MFSAM), which can segment surgical instruments with few training data. MFSAM adopts encoder-decoder architecture, where the encoder is the pre-trained network image encoder of SAM, and the decoder consists of gradient convolutional block and transposed convolution. The image encoder is utilized as the encoder, leveraging pre-trained knowledge to expedite convergence speed and mitigate training costs. By capturing semantic dependencies of gradient features through gradient convolutional blocks, emphasis is placed on target regions, thereby facilitating surgical instrument localization. Transposed convolution is employed for up-sampling feature maps to obtain refined edges. With fewer training samples, MFSAM achieves accurate segmentation of surgical instruments. The proposed network is evaluated on endovis 17, the results demonstrate that MFSAM achieves state-of-the-art performance. The proposed method can precise identification of corresponding surgical instruments while providing valuable auxiliary information to assist doctors in endoscopic surgery. © 2024 IEEE.},
